Probably should be `private final static ...` and moved at the very top of the class.

Generally, we prefer the structure of a Java class to be as follows:
```java
public class Foo {
   // first: static members (first public then private)
   public static final String FOO = "foo";

   private static final String BAR = "bar";
   
   public static void baz() {
      // then publicÎ static methods
   }

   // then private final members
   private final String qux;
   private final int abc;
   
   // then private mutable members
   private boolean def;

   public Foo() { 
      /* then constructor(s) */ 
   }

   public void callMe() { 
      /* then public methods (aka API of the class) */
   }

   public void callMeToo() { 
      // <...>
   }

   private String helperMethod() {
      /* then private helper methods */
   }
```

Note that it is not a requirement or rule, it's more of a recommendation. 

Especially when it comes to methods. For example, if the class is very big, like some RPC service implementation, it makes sense to keep related methods together, like `private String helperMethod()` methods closer to the place where they are used. Also, if you extend some `abstract class` you may want to override `protected abstract` methods immediately after the constructor, but before any public methods that you add after that, etc.

Fields (members), however, generally always stay on top.

