It looks something like this to me:
```java
/** Clean function. Can't crash. */
int main() {
    if (foo || bar) {
        return doThis();
    } else {
        return doThat();
    }
}

/** Suddenly, can crash if misused */
int doThis() {
    if (foo) {
       return a;
    } else if (bar) {
       return b;
    } else {
       throw new IllegalStateException();
    }
}
```

Nothing would throw exception in `main()`, but suddenly `doThis()` just happens to be implemented in a way that it is now possible to break the program by calling it from a wrong place.

I don't think it's a great design if new code suddenly introduces more options for a program to end up in a wrong state.

I think with this reorganization in place, just having each enum constant to override `prepareQuery` is the most readable version. You clearly see which CTEs are used in which mode and which filters are applied in which mode.

I am super confused with this hierarchy. Every single method here exposes implementation details of how reporting works instead of concealing them behind a single simple entrypoint. This is how it should like:
```java
interface Report {
    record Output(String uri) {}

    Output generateReport(ReportRequest request);
    // or maybe even more straightforward
    Output generateReport(Map<String, String> params);
}
```
Maybe one day we conver `Report.Output` into a proto. Literally everything else must be hidden in the implementation.

`HeavyNode` class should remain the same. If you need another Node that behaves differently but is also heavy, create new class, there is nothing wrong with it. That's exactly why `protected` method `weight()` exists: any node can override this method and return anything it wants, there is no need to stuff everything into the same class hierarchy just because you want all those nodes to be `HEAVY`:

```java
class A extends SimpleNode<Foo> {
    @Override
    public Weight weight(PolyflowExecContext ctx) {
      return Weight.HEAVY;
    }

    public ExecutionResult doExecute(PolyflowExecContext ctx) {
        return Foo.newBuilder().build();
    }
}

class B extends SimpleNode<Bar> {
    @Override
    public Weight weight(PolyflowExecContext ctx) {
      return Weight.HEAVY;
    }

    public ExecutionResult doExecute(PolyflowExecContext ctx) {
        return Bar.newBuilder().setBar(123456).build();
    }
}

class C extends SimpleNode<Baz> {
    @Override
    public Weight weight(PolyflowExecContext ctx) {
      return Weight.HEAVY;
    }

    public ExecutionResult doExecute(PolyflowExecContext ctx) {
        return Baz.newBuilder().setData("aaa").build();
    }
}
```

all of the above is absolutely OK. 

